Titre$Lycoperdon Ellis 1990
com$adapt GF sept 2014

rem$LANGUE$Anglais
1,0$source : Fungi Without Gills: Hymenomycetes and Gasteromycetes : An Indentification Handbook de M.B. Ellis et J.Pamela Ellis (juin 1990) 
1,0$


1,0$On wood, spores smooth$pyriforme
1,2$On soil, spores not smooth 	
2,0$Outer wall of fruitbody with few or no spines, common on coastal dunes$lividum
2,3$Outer wall with numerous spines, not common on coastal dunes ..
3,0$Spines 3-6 mm long$echinatum
3,4$Spines never more than 3 mm long and usually much less
4,5$Spores with attached pedicels
4,7$Spores without attached pedicels
5,0$Pedicels 10-35 long$pedicellatum
5,6$Pedicels much shorter
6,0$Pedicels 2-3 long, spines on fruitbody dropping off readily leaving a layer of brown sphaerocysts$marginatum
6,0$Pedicels 0.5-1.5 long, spines slender, persistent$umbrinum
7,0$Fruitbody when young covered with large, white or creamy veil remnants which persist and form a ring-like zone around base of head$mammaefonne
7,8$No such veil remnants
8,9$Spines dark, often blackish
8,10$Spines paler, never blackish
9,0$Spines dropping off, leaving areolate patches$foetidum
9,0$Spines persistent, no a reo late patches$atropurpureum
10,11$Spores coarsely warted, warts often scattered
10,12$Spores minutely and closely warted
11,0$Fruitbodies whitish, growing on dry, calcareous grassland .$decipiens
11,0$Fruitbodies mostly creamy-coffee coloured, growing in woods, quite common$molle
12,0$Subgleba purplish brown, a rare species found on damp, acid soil, e.g. moors with Sphagnum$ericaeum
12,0$Subgleba white to brown, very common in conifer and mixed woods and often also elsewhere$perlatum
